# Handles: the small numbers a program holds for the host's objects (buffers, maps, sets; files and
# listings in stage 3), numbered from 1 and never reused, so a handle used after it was closed or
# after its region was released traps naming its kind, as `refined run` does. A region lists the
# handles it owns and closes them when it is released.
#
# This module is generated from tools/handles.dsl by tools/build.py.

handles is module
handles purpose "Handles: the small numbers a program holds for the host's objects, the region that owns each, and what is given back when an object is closed or its region released"
handles path handles
handles imports standard.machine
handles imports standard.system
handles imports standard.string
handles imports standard.math
handles imports standard.compare
handles imports standard.logic
handles imports standard.test
handles imports frames
handles imports heap
handles imports tables
handles exports newHandle
handles exports objectOf
handles exports kindOf
handles exports regionOf
handles exports drop
handles exports dropRegion

memoryAccess is capability
memoryAccess purpose "Authority to keep the table of handles and each region's list of them in memory"
memoryAccess grants use machine.memory none

systemCalls is capability
systemCalls purpose "Authority to take memory for the handles and give their objects back, and to end the process on a trap"
systemCalls grants use system.calls none

# As in host.py and the C runtime: handles are numbered from 1 into one table and never reused, so a handle used
# after it was closed, or after the region that owns it was released, is caught and traps naming its kind.
# machine.state bytes 256 to 319 belong to this module: 256 the address of the table, 264 how many entries it
# holds, 272 the next handle (0 before the first). An entry is 24 bytes: at 0 its kind (0 when closed), 8 the
# address of its object, 16 the region that owns it (0 for none).
# A region's header (regions) keeps its handles at 48, the address of a list (at 0 how many it holds, then the
# handles), and at 56 how many are on it.
# The objects (heap blocks): a Buffer, at 0 its bytes, 8 how many, 16 how many the block holds, 24 a list of the
# copies `toBytes` handed out, 32 how many, 40 how many the list holds; a Map, at 0 its region, 8 whether its
# values are text, 16 its table of keys and values, 104 its table of the text keys copied into its region; a Set,
# its table at 0.
tableAt is constant
tableAt purpose "Where the address of the table of handles is kept, in machine.state"
tableAt type Int64
tableAt value 256

capacityAt is constant
capacityAt purpose "Where the number of entries the table holds is kept, in machine.state"
capacityAt type Int64
capacityAt value 264

nextAt is constant
nextAt purpose "Where the next handle is kept, in machine.state"
nextAt type Int64
nextAt value 272

entryBytes is constant
entryBytes purpose "The bytes of an entry of the table of handles"
entryBytes type Int64
entryBytes value 24

kindBuffer is constant
kindBuffer purpose "The kind of a byte buffer"
kindBuffer type Int64
kindBuffer value 3

kindMap is constant
kindMap purpose "The kind of a map"
kindMap type Int64
kindMap value 4

kindSet is constant
kindSet purpose "The kind of a set"
kindSet type Int64
kindSet value 5

